WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.130
A private AI assistant is an application that uses models, data, interfaces,

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:10.998
and services controlled by a person or organization under defined security and privacy rules.

00:00:10.998 --> 00:00:16.028
It may run on a local computer, private server, isolated cloud instance,

00:00:16.028 --> 00:00:17.285
or hybrid architecture.

00:00:17.285 --> 00:00:19.381
A chatbot mainly exchanges messages.

00:00:19.381 --> 00:00:23.572
An assistant applies instructions and context to help with tasks.

00:00:23.572 --> 00:00:26.087
A knowledge assistant retrieves approved sources.

00:00:26.087 --> 00:00:31.955
An agent can select tools and perform actions, creating greater capability and greater risk.

00:00:31.955 --> 00:00:36.146
Private does not simply mean the model file is local.

00:00:36.146 --> 00:00:40.757
Prompts, documents, chat history, retrieval indexes, speech services, analytics, logs, backups,

00:00:40.757 --> 00:00:45.368
update channels, and remote access can all move or expose data.

00:00:45.368 --> 00:00:49.140
Local systems can improve control, offline capability, latency, customization,

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:50.397
and cost predictability.

00:00:50.397 --> 00:00:55.427
Hosted services may offer stronger models, managed scaling, and less infrastructure work.

00:00:55.427 --> 00:00:59.619
Hybrid systems can route workloads according to sensitivity and capability.

00:00:59.619 --> 00:01:00.876
Control creates responsibility.

00:01:00.876 --> 00:01:06.325
The operator must size hardware, select models, maintain runtimes, secure APIs, protect data,

00:01:06.325 --> 00:01:12.193
evaluate behavior, recover failures, and decide when the assistant should defer to a human.

00:01:12.193 --> 00:01:16.804
A successful assistant is a practical system, not a model demo.

00:01:16.804 --> 00:01:21.414
It has a narrow purpose, measurable value, usable interface, grounded information,

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:25.186
controlled access, reliable operations, and limits that users understand.

00:01:25.186 --> 00:01:27.701
Draw the trust boundary before building.

00:01:27.701 --> 00:01:32.312
Mark users, browsers, model servers, document stores, databases, tools, remote APIs,

00:01:32.312 --> 00:01:34.408
administrators, backups, and update sources.

00:01:34.408 --> 00:01:38.180
Every crossing needs an explicit data and access decision.
